<DocsLayout title="Hosting" description="Share your singleplayer world or a Paper server.">
  <h2>Host a singleplayer world</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open your world, then press <kbd>G</kbd> or click <strong>etmc</strong> in the pause menu, and choose
      <strong>Host this world</strong>.</li>
    <li>If you haven't added a relay yet, paste one in. <strong>Find a relay</strong> opens a list of public
      ones.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Start hosting</strong>. etmc copies your invite link for you.</li>
    <li>Send the link to your friends.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="warn"><strong>Your secret isn't protected.</strong> It's saved as plain text in <code>config/etmc.json</code>, and anyone with your link can read it and join. Keep it random, and never use a password you
    use anywhere else.</div>
  <p>Keep the world open while friends are playing. Leaving the world stops hosting. To copy the link again, open
    <strong>Session status</strong> and click <strong>Copy invite link</strong>, or run <code>/etmc invite</code>.</p>
  <p>etmc picks a random network name and secret. To see or change them, click <strong>More options</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Let offline accounts join</h2>
  <p>Minecraft checks each player's account. To let offline accounts join your world, click <strong>More options</strong>
    and turn on <strong>Offline accounts</strong> before you click <strong>Start hosting</strong>. Stopping hosting turns the
    check back on. On a Paper server, set <code>online-mode=false</code> in <code>server.properties</code> instead.</p>
  <div class="warn"><strong>Names aren't checked with this on.</strong> Anyone with your link can join under any name.
    Friends with real accounts start with a new inventory, and get their old one back when it's off.</div>

  <h2>Show who's inviting</h2>
  <p>
    Under <strong>More options</strong>, turn on <strong>Sign as me</strong> before you click
    <strong>Start hosting</strong>, and your link shows
    "&lt;your name&gt; invites you to join their world". Players can confirm that with Mojang, so nobody else can use
    your name on a link. It's off unless you turn it on.
  </p>
  <p>
    etmc signs each player's connection with the key Minecraft uses for your chat, and players see your account. It
    needs a real Minecraft account, and a recent version of the game.
  </p>

  <h2>Host a Paper server</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Download the etmc Paper jar from <a href="https://github.com/L5Z12/etmc/releases">Releases</a> and put it in
      your <code>plugins</code> folder.</li>
    <li>Create a config with the <a href="/generator">generator</a>, which suggests a random secret, and save it as
      <code>plugins/etmc/config.yml</code>.</li>
    <li>Restart the server, or run <code>/etmc reload</code>.</li>
    <li>Copy the <code>etmc://</code> link from the server log, or run <code>/etmc link</code>.</li>
    <li>Share the link with your players.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="warn"><strong>Your secret isn't protected.</strong> It's plain text in <code>config.yml</code>, and anyone with your link can read it and join. If you leave the default <code>change-me</code>, etmc replaces it with a random one. Keep it random, and never use a password you
    use anywhere else.</div>
  <p>Running a public server that anyone can join? Leave the secret empty.</p>

  <h2>Keep your host key</h2>
  <p>
    Everyone with your link can read the secret, so the secret can't prove who the host is. Your link also holds a
    host key, which lets players check they've reached you and not someone posing as you. etmc makes the key the first
    time you host, and saves it in <code>config/etmc/host-key</code>, or <code>plugins/etmc/host-key</code> on a Paper
    server.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your links keep working as long as you keep that file, so back it up. If you lose it, or someone else gets a copy,
    delete it, host again and share a new link.
  </p>
  <div class="warn"><strong>Never share the host-key file.</strong> Anyone who has it can pose as you. Leave it out of
    modpacks and shared config folders.</div>
  <p>
    If someone takes your address on the network, your players will refuse to connect to them, but they may not be
    able to reach you either. Move to a new network name and secret, and share a new link.
  </p>

  <h2>Stop anyone taking your server's address</h2>
  <p>
    If players can reach your Paper server, set <code>public-address</code> in <code>config.yml</code>, for example
    <code>tcp://play.example.com:11010</code>, and open that port. Players then connect straight to your server, so
    nobody on the network can take its address from them, and you don't need a relay. Share the new link afterwards.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you share a config URL instead of a link, you can also move to a new network without players needing a new link.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
